WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.240
For native iOS developers, image management is synonymous with x-codes.xcassetscatalog.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:15.040
This system relies on the operating system to compile density-specific variants directly into the application binary at build time.

3
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.160
React Native removes this OS-level cataloging.

4
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.000
In its place, the JavaScript Bundler Metro handles asset resolution during the bundling process.

5
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:32.560
This shift replaces static, pre-compiled buckets, with a system where assets are sorted and routed dynamically into specific pipelines as they are requested.

6
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.880
The core technical difference is timing.

7
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:41.520
While native density inference is an OS-level build time process, React Native's density inference happens at runtime.

8
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.520
To navigate this, we can map common x-code UI elements directly to their equivalent metro configurations.

9
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.160
This architectural matrix illustrates the difference.

10
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.440
On the left, x-code provides a rigid tree structure for 1x, 2x, and 3x slots.

11
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.440
On the right, Metro looks at a standard directory of raw PNG files.

12
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.920
x-code requires you to manually assign files to specific slots.

13
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:12.560
Metro uses a flat file structure, where variants with at 2x and at 3x suffixes sit alongside the base image in the same folder.

14
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:19.760
When Metro bundles the app, it scans these directory structures and groups the adjacent variants into a unified asset map.

15
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:30.320
Understanding this transition from pre-compilation to runtime resolution is the first step in diagnosing why assets might look blurry or fail to appear in a cross-platform environment.

16
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:39.120
The React Native require function acts as the trigger for Metro's resolution logic, signaling the bundler to find the correct file paths during the build.

17
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:45.760
The actual selection of which file to display occurs later, when the image component is rendered on the physical device.

18
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.560
This process starts with the image component code.

19
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:56.880
It queries pixel ratio.get, which identifies the hardware density, in this case a 3, and routes the request to the at 3x file.

20
00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.920
If the density is 2, the system instantly routes to the at 2x variant.

21
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.600
This runtime visibility creates a specific failure mode.

22
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:11.760
Unlike x-code, which warns you about missing density slots at built time, Metro fails silently.

23
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.240
If the at 3x file is missing, the app will still build without errors.

24
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:25.760
However, at runtime, the system will find no match for the high density request and fall back to upscaling the 1x asset, leading to visible pixelation.

25
00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:32.240
This local resolution logic is distinct from how the framework handles remote assets using the URI format.

26
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:38.480
When you load an image from a remote server, the source bypasses the Metro Bundler's inference engine entirely.

27
00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:45.680
Because Metro cannot inspect the metadata of a file that hasn't been fetched yet, you must provide explicit width and height styles.

28
00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.000
Without them, the image has no calculated size, and will not appear.

29
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.920
This code demonstrates a URI source with explicit dimensions.

30
00:02:53.920 --> 00:03:00.640
It also shows how to include an authorization header required for fetching assets from protected endpoints.

31
00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.520
Because the system is dynamic, it requires more active validation from the developer

32
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.000
to ensure that every density variant is present before the app is shipped.

33
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:18.160
For app icons in a bare React Native project, you manually update the iOS app icon directory within xcode structure.

34
00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:26.800
Expo abstracts this through the app.json configuration file, removing the need to interact with the native asset catalog directly.

35
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:36.000
Instead of managing dozens of files, define a single 1024 by 1024 master icon in app.json.

36
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:43.520
During the build, Expo takes this master file and automatically generates the entire matrix of required iOS point sizes,

37
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.920
slotting them into the native catalog.

38
00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:53.360
This translation also applies to the native iOS launch screen, which is traditionally a storyboard or an image set in Xcode.

39
00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:59.920
In React Native, this is replaced by the Expo splash screen module and its corresponding parameters in app.json.

40
00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.120
This shifts the responsibility for dismissing the splash screen.

41
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.800
Rather than being tied to the native app delegate setup, it is now controlled programmatically through JavaScript.

42
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:14.480
Using the hide async function allows you to keep the splash screen visible until your asynchronous data fetching,

43
00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.240
like a user profile or configuration is actually complete.

44
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:24.640
Moving this event to the JavaScript thread ensures the splash screen dismisses only when internal logic is ready,

45
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.440
rather than relying on native side timing.

46
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:35.520
Native iOS development often uses PDF-based vector sets within X assets to ensure icon scale without losing quality.

47
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.880
React Native cannot render these native PDF vectors.

48
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.000
To achieve the same effect, we use the SVG format as a cross-platform alternative.

49
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.520
We integrate SVGs directly into the asset pipeline.

50
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.280
Because Metro doesn't support SVG natively, we use the React Native SVG transformer.

51
00:04:53.280 --> 00:05:01.120
It intercepts the raw SVG file and converts it into an executable React component, making it behave like a standard UI element.

52
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:07.600
Custom fonts are another native asset, typically linked through the info.plist using the UI app fonts key.

53
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.400
In React Native, you can either load these fonts dynamically at runtime via Expo font,

54
00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:18.560
or use the React Native asset CLI to automate native injection into the iOS and Android projects.

55
00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:26.320
Finally, Xcode's data sets are used for binary files, like core ML models or AR reference data.

56
00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:33.280
To handle these in React Native, you update the asset Xs array in your metro.config.js.

57
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.640
This instructs Metro to treat these specific extensions as bundleable assets.

58
00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.280
Once bundled, Expo asset provides a local URI for the file, which can then be seamlessly

59
00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:48.640
passed into native machine learning frameworks, like TensorFlow Lite.

60
00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.240
This configuration layer allows Metro to replicate the functionality of native data sets

61
00:05:54.240 --> 00:06:00.160
for almost any binary file type. The translation from Excode to Metro is now complete.

62
00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.000
We have mapped density-aware images to require, icons and splash screens to app.json,

63
00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:11.360
and fonts and binaries to JavaScript side-loaders. Managing assets this way removes the

64
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:17.200
requirement to manually edit native XML or P list files for every new image or font.

65
00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.400
Even though we are configuring these through JavaScript and JSON, the end result on the device

66
00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.000
is an optimized asset utilizing the native hardware efficiently.

67
00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.920
The primary developer responsibility is to maintain the integrity of file naming conventions

68
00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:38.080
to avoid violent fallbacks at runtime. Mastering Metro's resolution logic allows software engineers

69
00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:48.720
to maintain a complete asset pipeline without leaving the JavaScript environment.

